  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "Is it safe?",
      "title": "Is this change safe?",
      "chapter": "setup",
      "body": "The checkout service applies discount codes. It reads and writes the orders database, charges cards through an external payment provider, and asks the Inventory team’s service whether items are in stock.\n\nA developer has just changed how discounts are rounded. Before shipping, the team checks it by hand: click through a few purchases on a test server, and it all looks fine.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Every change carries a question: what else did this break? Tests are how you answer it quickly, every time, without relying on anyone remembering what to check."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "1 day",
        "covers": "what someone clicks",
        "escaped": "often"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "By hand",
      "title": "Clicking around misses things",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Nobody tried a basket of three items at €9.99 with a 10% code. The new rounding charges €26.98 instead of €26.97 — a cent too much on thousands of orders before support notices.\n\nManual checks are slow, so they happen rarely, and they only cover what someone thought to try. The bug was found by users.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "The later a bug is found, the more it costs: seconds in a test, hours in review, days in production — plus refunds and trust."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Rounding bug shipped",
        "- Found by customers, not by the team"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "1 day",
        "covers": "what someone clicks",
        "escaped": "often"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "svc": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "si": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Unit tests",
      "title": "Unit tests: one piece, in isolation",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "A [[unit test]] calls one function with known inputs and checks the output — here, the total for a given basket and code. It runs in milliseconds, with no database and no network.\n\nBecause they’re so cheap, you write thousands, and they run on every change. The rounding case becomes one line that fails the moment someone breaks it again.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Write the test for a bug before you fix it: watch it fail, fix the code, watch it pass. That bug can never quietly come back."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Unit tests call Checkout directly",
        "- Real database, provider and Inventory"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "30 s",
        "covers": "logic only",
        "escaped": "fewer"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "svc": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "db": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "inv": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "si": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Test doubles",
      "title": "Test doubles stand in for the rest",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Checkout can’t run without its dependencies, so tests swap them for [[test doubles]]: a **fake** database that lives in memory, a **stub** provider that always answers “approved”, a **mock** that records whether a card was charged exactly once.\n\nThe catch: a double only knows what you told it. If the real provider renames a field, every unit test still passes.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Doubles make tests fast and repeatable, and they can quietly drift from reality. Something has to test against the real thing too."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Fake database, stub provider, stub Inventory"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "30 s",
        "covers": "logic only",
        "escaped": "fewer"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "inv": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "si": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Integration",
      "title": "Integration tests: the real neighbours",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "An [[integration test]] runs Checkout against a real database — started fresh in a container for the test run — and checks what actually lands in the tables. SQL typos, broken migrations and mismatched types show up here, not in production.\n\nThe payment provider stays stubbed (you can’t charge real cards), but you can call its sandbox in a separate, smaller suite.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Containers made this cheap: a throwaway PostgreSQL or Kafka takes seconds to start, so integration tests can run on every change too."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Fake database → real database in a container",
        "~ Tests take seconds, not milliseconds"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "4 min",
        "covers": "+ database queries",
        "escaped": "fewer"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "inv": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "si": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Contracts",
      "title": "Contract tests: between teams",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Checkout depends on Inventory, which another team owns and deploys on its own schedule. If they rename `in_stock` to `available`, Checkout’s stub never notices.\n\nA [[contract test]] writes down what Checkout expects — this request, a response with these fields — and Inventory’s own pipeline checks every change against it. A breaking change fails in *their* build, before it ships.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Tools like Pact record these consumer expectations. Contracts replace most of the slow end-to-end tests that would otherwise be needed between services."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Checkout’s expectations recorded as a contract",
        "+ Inventory verifies it on every change"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "4 min",
        "covers": "+ API contracts",
        "escaped": "rare"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "inv": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "sd": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "si": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "End-to-end",
      "title": "End-to-end tests: the whole journey",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "An [[end-to-end test]] drives the real system the way a user would: open the shop in a browser, add three items, enter a code, pay with a sandbox card, see the confirmation.\n\nIt is the only test that proves everything works together — and the slowest, most fragile one. A dozen of them for the journeys that make money is plenty.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "An end-to-end test tells you something broke, not what. When one fails, the cheaper tests below it should point to the cause."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ A browser test drives the whole system",
        "~ Minutes per run"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "15 min",
        "covers": "whole system",
        "escaped": "rare"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "sd": {
          "effect": "pulse",
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "db": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "inv": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "si": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Pyramid",
      "title": "The test pyramid",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Put together, the suites form a [[test pyramid]]: thousands of unit tests at the bottom, hundreds of integration and contract tests in the middle, a handful of end-to-end tests on top.\n\nLower layers are fast, precise and cheap to run on every change; higher ones are slow but closer to what users do. Push each check as low as it can go.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "An upside-down pyramid — mostly end-to-end tests — is a common trap: an hour per run, frequent random failures, and a team that stops trusting red builds."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "30 s → 15 min",
        "covers": "whole system",
        "escaped": "rare"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "sp-5": {
          "effect": "none"
        },
        "users-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "us-3": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "Flaky",
      "title": "Case: the flaky test",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "One end-to-end test fails about one run in twenty. It waits a fixed two seconds for the confirmation email, and sometimes the email takes three. People learn to press “re-run” — and when a real bug turns that test red, they re-run that too.\n\nA [[flaky test]] is worse than no test: it trains the team to ignore failures.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Fix the cause: wait for a condition instead of a fixed time, control the clock, give each test its own data, never depend on test order. Track flaky tests and fix or delete them — don’t hide them behind automatic retries."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Fails 1 run in 20 for no reason",
        "- Real failures get re-run and ignored"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "15 min",
        "covers": "whole system",
        "escaped": "often"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "us-3": {
          "effect": "comet",
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "si": {
          "effect": "none"
        },
        "users-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "tests-4": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ts-3": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "short": "Load & failure",
      "title": "Testing for load and for failure",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Correct isn’t enough. A **load test** sends production-like traffic to a copy of the system and finds the point where latency turns upward (see *Latency & Throughput*) before your users do.\n\n**Failure tests** break things on purpose — stop a database replica, slow the payment provider down — and check that timeouts, retries and failover behave as designed (see *Timeouts & Retries*).",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "The last layer runs in production itself: release to a few users first and watch the numbers. That is the canary in the next lesson."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Load test: 5× normal traffic on staging",
        "+ Failure test: payment provider slowed to 5 s"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "feedback": "hours · nightly",
        "covers": "+ load and failure",
        "escaped": "rare"
      },
